    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>This <strong>Stamina InMotion elliptical</strong> review covers the InMotion E1000 Compact Strider, one of the longest-running small elliptical trainers on the market. Unlike most under desk ellipticals, it is designed to be used standing. You can also sit and pedal it from a chair, which makes it one of the few machines that does both.</p>
<p>Based on specs and owner reviews, the E1000 is a good buy if you want a budget machine for standing use, or one machine you can use standing and seated. It is not the quietest option, and standing use needs something to hold for balance. If you will only ever use it seated, a dedicated seated elliptical is usually smoother and quieter.</p>

<h2>What Is the Stamina InMotion E1000?</h2>
<p>The Stamina InMotion E1000 is a compact strider: two foot platforms that glide in an elliptical path, a tension knob to adjust resistance, and a small monitor that shows basic workout stats. There are no handlebars and no motor. It is small enough to slide under a desk or store in a closet.</p>
<p>You will see it called the InMotion compact elliptical, the Stamina E1000, the "in motion E1000" or simply the Stamina InMotion elliptical trainer. They all describe the same machine. Check the [[amazon:stamina-inmotion-e1000|current Stamina InMotion E1000 listing]] for the latest specs, weight capacity and price.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Not the InMotion II:</strong> the Stamina InMotion II is a manual treadmill, a completely different product. If you were looking for a treadmill review, our <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-walking-pad/">under desk elliptical vs walking pad</a> and <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-treadmill/">under desk elliptical vs treadmill</a> comparisons cover walking options for a standing desk.</p></div>

<h2>Standing vs Seated: How the E1000 Works Both Ways</h2>
<p>This is the main reason people buy the E1000, so it is worth understanding how each mode feels.</p>
<h3>Using it standing</h3>
<p>Standing is what the E1000 is built for. You step onto the platforms and stride in place with a gliding motion, a bit like a very short elliptical stride without the handles. Because you carry your full body weight, it is noticeably harder work than seated pedaling and feels closer to walking.</p>
<p>The catch is balance. With no handlebars, you need to hold something stable: the edge of a standing desk, a kitchen counter or the back of a heavy chair. Most owners keep one hand on a surface, especially in the first few sessions. If balance is a concern for you, use it seated or choose a different machine.</p>
<h3>Using it seated</h3>
<p>You can also place it in front of a chair and pedal. This turns it into a seated elliptical similar to a Cubii, with the tension knob setting the effort. It works, but it is not what the machine was optimized for. Because it is built to stand on, it may sit taller than low-profile seated models, so knee clearance under a desk can be tighter.</p>
